MANUAL
------

GENERAL
-------

For this pretty little game you need
at least:
-a 486
-4 MByte of RAM
-500 KB of free conventional
 memory (rejoice, you DOS freaks)
-a CD-ROM drive
-a monitor (no joke!)
-a sense of black humor


THE IDEA
--------

The game is actually a strategy game
without strategy, in which you shoot
down aliens. Of course the cute little
strangers shouldn't simply be bumped
off for no reason! For the dirty work
you get money, which you can spend on
new armament as you please. The goal
is to survive all the levels and to
relocate the nasty Golem into eternal
darkness. Got it?


THE CONTROLS
------------

So far so good, but how do you steer
your funny little spaceship?
Well, there are four ways: the
keyboard, the joystick, the mouse and
telepathy.

The keyboard works like this:

    Forward          : Arrow up
    Backward         : Arrow down
    Left             : Arrow left
    Right            : Arrow right
    Fire             : Ctrl
    Fly high/low     : Alt
    Select bonus     : Space
    Fire bonus       : Ctrl+Space

    Pause            : P
    Esc              : Abort game

The joystick is used like this:

    Forward, backward,
    left, right      : Stick
    Fire             : Button1
    Fly high/low     : Button2

    Since most sticks only have 2
    buttons, you select and fire
    the bonuses with the keyboard:

    Select bonus     : -
    Fire bonus       : +

Here comes the mouse:

    Forward, backward,
    left, right      : Move the
                       mouse that
                       way

    Fire             : Button1
    Fly high/low     : Button3
    Select bonus     : Button2
    Fire bonus       : Button1+Button2


FLYING HIGH AND LOW
-------------------

You are surely wondering why you can
fly high or low. Here's why:
The aliens are either in the air or on
the ground, which you can tell by how
far away their shadow is. To crumble
an alien to bits, you have to fly at
the same height, which does raise the
risk of a collision quite a bit! So
watch out! By the way, there are also
homing weapon systems that aim at the
right height automatically...


THE BONUS SYSTEM
----------------

During the game, bonuses keep turning
up as a reward for disintegrated
enemies; you grab them by touching
them with your ship. You can also buy
bonuses in the shop. Your bonuses,
three at most, are listed at the
bottom left of the screen.
If you already have three bonuses and
pick up another one, it is activated
at once. The selected bonus has a
frame around it. How to select and
fire a bonus is described above, under
the controls. Bonuses are a useful
source of money, because you can also
sell them in the shop.


ENERGY, MONEY AND LIVES
-----------------------

There's no shoot 'em up without an
energy display! On the left edge of
the screen you'll find a frame with
two vertical bars in it. The upper one
shows your shield energy, which slowly
recharges. The better the shield, the
faster the shield energy recharges.
Damage goes to the shield first, but
once it is at zero, the lower bar
suffers: your life energy. It doesn't
recharge by itself. There is an Aid
bonus, though, which pumps both
energies back up, a kind of potency
pill.
The number of your lives and the money
you've collected are shown in the top
left corner. For Player2 everything is
on the right side.


THE SHOP
--------

The shop is not for buying painkillers
for the aliens, but for buying
yourself new equipment. For that you
need dough, cash, bucks, moolah,
bread, dinero, greenbacks, clams,
smackers, Benjamins, scratch or simply
money.
How the shop works you'll have to
figure out yourself, because typing a
description ten feet long bugs me like
crazy. But here are the most important
things:
-You can buy and sell equipment
-Which equipment, and how much, you
 can fit where depends on the jet;
 on the first jet, for example, you
 can't fit a ball
-A jet's standard weapon can't be
 sold
-Large pods can swap positions
-With two players you can share your
 money in the exchange menu
-You can page through the little
 windows at the bottom, which show
 all the junk
-When you sell, you only get half
 the price, for bonuses only a
 quarter
-You can only have two of the same
 large pod

DIFFERENT JETS
--------------

There's a nice choice of flying
machines in our game; our graphics guy
even conjured up a helicopter. The
better (more expensive) your jet, the
more positions you can use for fitting
weapons. Better jets also have more
life energy and ram the aliens out of
the way more easily. Most important:
the standard weapon keeps getting
better!


ENGINES AND SHIELDS
-------------------

Well, sh'yeah!
If you want to be more agile, buy
yourself a better engine! If the nasty
aliens keep giving you a beating, a
better shield is the order of the day:
it has more energy and recharges
faster!


SAVING/LOADING THE GAME
-----------------------

To save your game, you have to buy a
save bonus in the shop; the game is
then saved automatically. There is
only one single save! The save bonus
gets more expensive with every level!!
Have fun spending money!

MULTIPLAYER
-----------

It is possible to calm down your
whining brother, who is unhappy
because he can't play along. During
your own game, just click on
Multiplayer in the hangar, and the
little pest is ready to mess up your
game! You set his controls in the
options menu.
Once your fellow player has joined,
you can't throw him out again, so
think about it and choose wisely, for
only the penitent man will pass...
As mentioned, you can exchange money
with each other in the shop. Don't
fall for the illusion that it's twice
as easy with two, because the aliens
simply get stronger too. Oh well..


THE OPTIONS MENU
----------------

The options menu is self-explanatory.
If it's all Greek to someone, I can't
help them either.


WHAT TO DO IF THERE ARE PROBLEMS
--------------------------------

Yippee, I'm almost done describing.
If you have a problem, follow the
following following of follow-ups:
 -You notice a flicker in the bottom
  left corner of the screen.
 »Set the CPU again in the options
  menu in the hangar.
 -You have too little free
  conventional memory.
 »Ask a friend how to change
  Config.sys and Autoexec.bat to get
  more.
 -The game is suddenly hellishly
  slow when you use the joystick.
 »There's nothing I can do, just use
  the keys...
  (It works with Urs's joystick!)
 -Dark Planet quits with the Windows
  message 'Must be run in full
  screen'.
 »Run Dark Planet from the autorun
  of the original CD.

That's it then, have fun!


